{{define "content"}}
<vox-hero eyebrow="OpenVox Console" heading="Your whole fleet, in one place" data-i18n-attr="heading,eyebrow">
  <p data-i18n>
    See every node, decide what runs where, deploy your code, and act on
    what comes back. A Puppet Enterprise console for OpenVox &mdash; one
    Go binary, on top of the openvox-server and openvoxdb you already run.
  </p>
  <vox-cta slot="actions" href="#highlights" data-i18n>See what it does</vox-cta>
  <vox-cta slot="actions" href="https://github.com/avitacco/openvox-console" target="_blank" data-i18n>Get it on GitHub</vox-cta>
</vox-hero>

{{screenshot "dashboard"}}

<vox-grid min="200px" gap="lg">
  <vox-stat value="1" label="binary to deploy" data-i18n-attr="label"></vox-stat>
  <vox-stat value="2" label="architectures, built on every push" data-i18n-attr="label"></vox-stat>
  <vox-stat value="GPL-3.0" label="free and open source" data-i18n-attr="label"></vox-stat>
</vox-grid>

{{/* The five things the console is chosen for, in the order they are
     argued for. Each links to its capability page and to the guide that
     does it; nodes and classification - what any console has to do -
     follow in a compact list after them. */}}
<section class="site-section" id="highlights">
  <h2 class="vox-ts-xxl" data-i18n>Why teams choose it</h2>
  <div class="vox-prose">
    <p data-i18n>
      Every picture below is the console itself, captured from a running
      instance.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<vox-billboard class="highlight" heading="Know what is vulnerable, across the fleet" data-i18n-attr="heading">
  <div slot="media">{{screenshot "vulnerabilities"}}</div>
  <p data-i18n>
    Every package every node reports, matched against known
    vulnerabilities from OSV &mdash; or your own mirror of it, for sites
    with no way out &mdash; and from Tenable, merged into one list. See
    what patching would fix first. A node nobody could assess is shown as
    exactly that, never as clean.
  </p>
  <vox-cta slot="actions" href="features/security.html" data-i18n>Vulnerability tracking</vox-cta>
  <vox-cta slot="actions" href="guides/vulnerabilities.html" data-i18n>Set up a provider</vox-cta>
</vox-billboard>

<vox-billboard class="highlight" reverse heading="Sign in with the identity provider you already use" data-i18n-attr="heading">
  <div slot="media">{{screenshot "roles"}}</div>
  <p data-i18n>
    Single sign-on through OpenID Connect, with your provider's groups
    mapped onto console roles at every sign-in. Roles are plain sets of
    permissions, so people see exactly what their job needs. Automation
    gets service tokens of its own, and every change is on the record.
  </p>
  <vox-cta slot="actions" href="features/access-control.html" data-i18n>Access control</vox-cta>
  <vox-cta slot="actions" href="guides/access.html" data-i18n>Connect your provider</vox-cta>
</vox-billboard>

<vox-billboard class="highlight" heading="One binary that scales out when you need it" data-i18n-attr="heading">
  <div slot="media">{{screenshot "fleet-status"}}</div>
  <p data-i18n>
    Start with one instance. When the fleet grows, run the same image as
    separate web, worker and orchestrator instances, clustered with
    mutual TLS, and put classification right beside your compilers.
    Everything durable lives in Postgres, so instances come and go behind
    a load balancer, and one page shows the whole fleet of them.
  </p>
  <vox-cta slot="actions" href="features/scaling.html" data-i18n>Scaling</vox-cta>
  <vox-cta slot="actions" href="guides/cluster.html" data-i18n>Run more instances</vox-cta>
</vox-billboard>

<vox-billboard class="highlight" reverse heading="Run Puppet now, on the nodes you choose" data-i18n-attr="heading">
  <div slot="media">{{screenshot "jobs-list"}}</div>
  <p data-i18n>
    Run Puppet on one node, a selection, or every node a group matches,
    without waiting for the next scheduled run. Each node's result comes
    back on its own &mdash; status, output and a link to the report it
    produced &mdash; and a node that was offline says so straight away.
  </p>
  <vox-cta slot="actions" href="features/orchestration.html" data-i18n>Orchestration</vox-cta>
  <vox-cta slot="actions" href="guides/run-jobs.html" data-i18n>Run jobs</vox-cta>
</vox-billboard>

<vox-billboard class="highlight" heading="See what your code deploys actually reach" data-i18n-attr="heading">
  <div slot="media">{{screenshot "code-repositories"}}</div>
  <p data-i18n>
    Deploy from one control repository or one per team, on demand or on
    every push. Then see, for each repository, how many nodes are
    assigned to its environments against how many are actually running
    from them &mdash; the gap that says a deploy has not landed, or that
    nodes are running code nothing sends them to.
  </p>
  <vox-cta slot="actions" href="features/code.html" data-i18n>Code deployment</vox-cta>
  <vox-cta slot="actions" href="guides/deploy-code.html" data-i18n>Deploy code</vox-cta>
</vox-billboard>

<section class="site-section" id="capabilities">
  <h2 class="vox-ts-xxl" data-i18n>And the rest of a console</h2>
</section>

<vox-link-hub>
  <vox-link-hub-item href="features/nodes.html" heading="Nodes and inventory" data-i18n-attr="heading">
    <vox-icon slot="icon" name="plug"></vox-icon>
    <span data-i18n>Every node, what it last reported, whether it is reachable, and the state of its certificate.</span>
  </vox-link-hub-item>

  <vox-link-hub-item href="features/classification.html" heading="Classification" data-i18n-attr="heading">
    <vox-icon slot="icon" name="node-group"></vox-icon>
    <span data-i18n>Rule-based node groups that decide what configuration applies where.</span>
  </vox-link-hub-item>
</vox-link-hub>

<section class="site-section">
  <h2 class="vox-ts-xxl" data-i18n>Built on the stack you already run</h2>
  <div class="vox-prose">
    <p data-i18n>
      openvox-server still compiles catalogs and runs the CA; openvoxdb
      still stores facts and reports. Your agents carry on talking to
      exactly what they talk to now &mdash; the console serves the
      standard ENC contract, so there is nothing to change on the node
      side.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<vox-callout variant="tip" heading="Try it on a throwaway stack first" data-i18n-attr="heading">
  <p data-i18n-html>
    <code>docker compose up</code> brings up openvoxserver, openvoxdb,
    Postgres and the console together, so you can point a test agent at it
    and see your own nodes appear before committing to anything.
  </p>
</vox-callout>
{{end}}
